====================================================================================================
C1 EXPERIMENT — FORECAST ACCURACY METRICS — METHODOLOGY
====================================================================================================
This log explains how every number in
  results/c1_experiment/architecture1/metrics_architecture1.log
  results/c1_experiment/architecture2/metrics_architecture2.log
was computed, what data was used, and which formulas were applied.
====================================================================================================


1. INPUT DATA
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
For each architecture we have one CSV with three columns:

    sensor_timestamp, forecasted_value, actual_value

Per architecture:
  - results/c1_experiment/architecture1/forecast_vs_actual_architecture1.csv  (3 462 rows)
  - results/c1_experiment/architecture2/forecast_vs_actual_architecture2.csv  (3 540 rows)

How each CSV was built
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
The coordinator service (coordinator_service.py for Arch 1, pulsarConsumer60Batches.py for Arch 2)
writes two pandas DataFrame prints to its log on every minute boundary:

  1) "Actual values fetched from database" — 60 rows
        sensor_id  sensor_energy_value  sensor_timestamp  message_delay
        These are the 60 actuals that were just inserted into Postgres
        (model input for the next minute's prediction).

  2) "Forecasted values:" — 60 rows
        sensor_id  sensor_energy_value_prediction  sensor_timestamp
        These are the 60 predictions for the NEXT minute.

We scanned the per-architecture coordinator log line-by-line:
  - On "Forecasted values:"            -> open a "forecast" block; collect 60 rows
  - On "Actual values fetched ..."     -> open an "actual"   block; collect 60 rows
  - Skip any column-header line (it contains the string "sensor_id")
  - End the block on the next empty line

The captured tables were then merged by `sensor_timestamp` (inner join):

    forecasts:  {sensor_timestamp -> forecasted_value}
    actuals:    {sensor_timestamp -> actual_value}
    paired   :  forecasts ∩ actuals    (this is what populates the CSV)

Boundary effects:
  - First minute (00:00:00 .. 00:00:59):    there's no prior forecast to compare against,
    so those 60 actuals end up "only-in-actuals" and are not in the paired CSV.
  - Last minute  (01:00:00 .. 01:00:59 or 01:01:00..01:01:59):
    the final forecast cycle predicts beyond the run window, so those 60 predictions
    end up "only-in-forecasts" and are not in the paired CSV.

That explains why Arch 1 has 3 462 paired rows (not exactly 3 600) and Arch 2 has 3 540.


2. METRIC DEFINITIONS AND FORMULAS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
For n paired observations of actual y_i and prediction ŷ_i:

  MAE   (Mean Absolute Error)        = (1/n) · Σ |y_i − ŷ_i|
                                        units: same as y                  (here: W)

  MSE   (Mean Squared Error)         = (1/n) · Σ (y_i − ŷ_i)²
                                        units: y²                         (here: W²)

  RMSE  (Root Mean Squared Error)    = √MSE
                                        units: same as y                  (here: W)

  MAPE  (Mean Absolute Percentage Error)
                                     = (100/n) · Σ |y_i − ŷ_i| / |y_i|
                                        units: %
                                        guard: rows with y_i = 0 are excluded
                                               (Arch 1: 0 excluded; Arch 2: 0 excluded).

  sMAPE (Symmetric MAPE, reference only)
                                     = (100/n) · Σ |y_i − ŷ_i| / ((|y_i| + |ŷ_i|) / 2)
                                        units: %
                                        guard: rows where |y_i| + |ŷ_i| = 0 are excluded.

  Supplementary "informal" relative magnitude:
      MAE ÷ mean(y)                  = MAE / ((1/n) · Σ y_i)
                                        units: %
                                        Closer to MAPE in spirit but uses a single mean
                                        rather than per-row normalization.  Useful when
                                        the actuals have a wide dynamic range.

Supporting statistics on the same series:
  actual_mean        = (1/n) · Σ y_i
  predicted_mean     = (1/n) · Σ ŷ_i
  actual_std         = pop std-dev of y_i
  predicted_std      = pop std-dev of ŷ_i
  pred_std/act_std   = ratio that indicates whether predictions "move with"
                       reality (1.0 = same variability; >1 = predictor overshoots).
  mean residual      = mean(y - ŷ)
                       positive => model under-predicts on average
                       negative => model over-predicts on average


3. EXACT PROCEDURE (Python sketch)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The full implementation lives in the Bash snippets in the conversation log,
but the core is the following Python:

    import csv, math, statistics

    actuals, predictions = [], []
    with open(csv_path) as f:
        for r in csv.DictReader(f):
            actuals.append(float(r["actual_value"]))
            predictions.append(float(r["forecasted_value"]))

    n = len(actuals)
    abs_errs = [abs(a - p) for a, p in zip(actuals, predictions)]
    sq_errs  = [(a - p) ** 2 for a, p in zip(actuals, predictions)]
    pct_errs = [abs(a - p) / abs(a) for a, p in zip(actuals, predictions) if a != 0.0]

    smape_vals = [
        abs(a - p) / ((abs(a) + abs(p)) / 2.0)
        for a, p in zip(actuals, predictions)
        if (abs(a) + abs(p)) > 0
    ]

    mae   = statistics.mean(abs_errs)
    mse   = statistics.mean(sq_errs)
    rmse  = math.sqrt(mse)
    mape  = 100.0 * statistics.mean(pct_errs)        # safe: pct_errs already excludes y_i=0
    smape = 100.0 * statistics.mean(smape_vals)

    actual_mean = statistics.mean(actuals)
    rel_mae     = 100.0 * mae / actual_mean


