5. WHY NEITHER ARCHITECTURE'S OWN actualvalues TABLE IS USED AS GROUND TRUTH
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
At first glance one might JOIN forecastedvalues with actualvalues per-architecture in Postgres
and compute MAE/RMSE/MAPE from there.  We deliberately do NOT do that here, because:

  - Arch 1's actualvalues table contains imputed (mean-filled) rows wherever Flink dropped
    out-of-order events.  Comparing arch 1's forecast against those imputed rows would give
    an unrealistically optimistic MAE (the prediction is being scored against an imputed
    proxy, not against reality).
  - Arch 2's actualvalues table contains only real events but boundary-shifted by 1 second
    (first message lost to startup race; see scripts/start_app_no_flink.sh comments).

Instead, each architecture is scored against the actuals THAT IT ACTUALLY OBSERVED through
its own coordinator log (the "Actual values fetched from database" block right before each
"Forecasted values:" block).  This:
  - applies the same scoring contract to both architectures,
  - exposes mean-fill behaviour in arch 1's metrics (the model is graded on what the
    pipeline really stored),
  - matches the operational reality each architecture would expose to a downstream consumer.

If a stricter, ground-truth-against-original-CSV comparison is desired, it is straightforward
to JOIN the paired CSVs against a sorted version of the disordered source CSV — that is left
as a follow-up "ground-truth-vs-internal-truth" experiment, not required for C1 as defined.


6. INTERPRETATION NOTES
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  • MAE penalises absolute misses linearly; RMSE penalises large misses quadratically.
    RMSE >> MAE indicates a long-tailed error distribution (some catastrophic misses).
    Arch 1: RMSE / MAE = 1.525  -> moderate outliers
    Arch 2: RMSE / MAE = 1.995  -> heavier outliers

  • MAPE is sensitive to small actuals (large %-error when y_i is small).  For this
    workload the actuals never reach zero, so MAPE is safe; sMAPE is reported as a
    secondary check that doesn't blow up when y_i is small.

  • The "mean residual" sign tells you the direction of bias:
        Arch 1 mean residual = -119.08 W  (model over-predicts)
        Arch 2 mean residual = -195.61 W  (model over-predicts more strongly)
    Both architectures over-shoot on average; arch 2 over-shoots more.

  • pred_std / actual_std > 1 means the predictor's series moves *more* than the
    real signal.  Both architectures exceed 1.0 — arch 2 by a large margin (2.29x).
    This is consistent with the model being asked to predict at amplitudes it never
    saw during training (MinMaxScaler fit on first 300 rows only — see
    model/train_model/initial_train.py:62-85 for the model definition).


